Here are the report narratives as a JSON list. Group them into themes following the rules.

[
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50949318",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Representative (rep) reported that the patient noticed not having pain relief about 2 weeks prior to seeing the physician. The Clinic took x-ray of the knee which showed both leads were severed. The patient is planning to get a Total Knee Arthroplasty (TKA) - which the Nalu system will be explanted at that time. At this time, there is no revision or explant scheduled.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.It is unknown if the device has been explanted. The reported device was not returned for evaluation; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50948697",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Representative (rep) reported that the trial lead fractured upon removal. The physician left the electrode portion in and was not removed, as he is not sure whether it is the lateral or medial superior genicular lead that fractured. There was no reported patient harm or injury.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The rep provided photos that show a fracture of the lead. The reported device was not returned for evaluation; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50947936",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Representative (rep) reported that the patient came in for an RCT follow up and mentioned a \"burning\" sensation when he would use one of his programs. The rep checked impedances, and the report showed a high impedance/error on electrode A3. Ran impedance report multiple times, closed out of programmer and also shut down disc, impedance report still remained high on electrode A3. Program 2 is the only program that utilizes electrode A3. Deleted Program 2 from patient's list of programs, patient will let the clinic know if he begins to experience any other \"burning\" sensations while utilizing his other programs. Follow up with the patient who indicated that he continues to do very well and has not experienced any burning sensation since reprogramming. There was no reported patient harm or injury.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The patient continues to do very well and has not experienced any burning sensation since reprogramming. The reported device remains implanted; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50937584",
  "narrative": "The patient reported a stitch was eroding from the implant site. The patient went to the hospital due to pain at the suture site and was given guidance on how to move forward with imaging. On (b)(6) 2026, X-Rays were performed, migration was not suspected, and it was noted the patient did not have stiches poking through their skin. However, the patient had bulging at the pocket site where the coils and stitches were. A revision procedure was performed where the physician buried the stitches and coil deeper under the skin to reduce patient discomfort. No further issues have been reported.\n\nThe Surgical Issue Questionnaire was reviewed for potential causes of the reported issue. Based on this review, implanting the device off-label, not performing an X-Ray immediately after the procedure to confirm device placement, inadequate fixation, the patient having recently gone through an MRI, implanting the device after the expiration date, not prepping the surface of the skin with an antiseptic solution, using incorrect tools, and not prescribing antibiotics pre-operatively have been ruled out. Other potential causes of the reported issue include severe force applied to the implant and excessive twisting or stretching.A Curonix Representative conducted a review of sterilization and packaging records for the respective product lot; Curonix has confirmed that the product was delivered sterile, validated sterilization parameters were used, and sterile barriers were verified to be intact following packaging. The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the reported issue is unknown. The investigation findings do not lead to a clear conclusion about the cause of the reported issue. Therefore, conclusion has been selected as unable to determine root cause.Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Surgical Issues. Surgical Issue rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required at this time. Surgical Issue rates will continue to be tracked and trended."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50936648",
  "narrative": "The patient reported their incision re-opened and was now infected. Antibiotics were prescribed and an explant procedure was performed on (b)(6) 2026.\n\nThe Surgical Issue Questionnaire was reviewed for potential causes of the reported issue. Based on this review, implanting the device after the expiration date, improperly closing the surgical site, and using incorrect tools have been ruled out. Additionally, the patient having contraindicating conditions and severe force to the implant procedure have been ruled out. However, the patient scratched the incision due to itching, which caused the wound to open up and became infected. A Curonix Representative conducted a review of sterilization and packaging records for the respective product lot; Curonix has confirmed that the product was delivered sterile, validated sterilization parameters were used, and sterile barriers were verified to be intact following packaging. The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the reported issue is due to Patient Non-Compliance as the patient scratched the incision (User Error-Patient). Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Surgical Issues. Surgical Issue rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required at this time. Surgical Issue rates will continue to be tracked and trended."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50928010",
  "narrative": "It was reported that the patient had their device explanted due to the leads becoming exposed and infected. The patient had received a cancer diagnosis and experienced significant weight loss since device implantation. The physician successfully explanted the device in its entirety, and no complications were reported.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. This incident is being re-opened/updated per PCAR42 remediation activities. No device was returned as the device was discarded at time of explant; therefore, no devices were available for return to the manufacturer for failure analysis. Based on the available information and in the absence of device return, the root cause of the reported clinical observations cannot be established."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50901693",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Representative (rep) reported that the patient reported an infection after lead pull and was prescribed antibiotics for 5 days.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The reported device was not returned for evaluation. No further intervention was required and the patient moved forward with a permanent system implant with no reported complications."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50900578",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Representative (rep) reported that the patient is suspected of having an infection at the incision site. The patient was prescribed antibiotics and planned to follow up with the physician at the surgical facility. Follow up found that the patient is reported as having the incision healed, open to air, and has resumed therapy.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The reported device remains implanted. Infection of the incision site is a known inherent risk of surgical procedures, however in this case it is unclear if there was an infection or if the site was possibly irritated by the patient's undergarments."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50899606",
  "narrative": "During implant of a Nalu device, after physician made the incision, when attempted to use introducer, they felt resistance and noticed there was a slit/tear on the introducer needle. A new introducer was used to complete the procedure.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. This incident is being re-opened/updated per PCAR42 remediation activities. The Introducer was received for failure analysis. Visual inspection by engineering confirmed that the tip of the introducer sheath was bent and that the needle had been pushed through the wall of the sheath. The investigation findings do not lead to a clear conclusion about the cause of the reported event; therefore, the most probable cause was unable to be established."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50899604",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Representative (rep) reported that during implantation that the physician decided to change the lead to a longer one and while he was trying to extract the second lead and stated that he needed to use hand-over-hand even pressure to get it out, or he could hydro-dissect using saline. He continued to pull, and eventually the lead broke at the point on the lead where the tines were deployed. The doctor was trying to figure out how to retrieve the broken end of the lead. The Rep suggested that if the patient did not need MRI, then it might be best to leave as is, rather than cause more trauma. The implantation was completed with the second device. There was no reported patient harm or injury.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The reported device was returned for evaluation. Visual inspection by engineering confirmed that the lead is damaged, the distal end of the outer pellethane has been removed, leaving the internal wiring. Therefore, a review and analysis of all available information indicated the most probable cause stems from the physician's misuse of product and surgical planning."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50886588",
  "narrative": "The patient was taken to the hospital on (b)(6) 2026 due to swelling in the lower extremity and concerns of infection. Antibiotics were prescribed the week prior after a follow up appointment with the implanting clinician. As of (b)(6) 2026, the patient completed their antibiotics, they are doing well, and resumed using the neurostimulator without issue.\n\nThe Other Adverse Events Issues Issue Questionnaire was reviewed for causes of the reported issue. Based on this review, implanting the device off-label, implanting the device too close to the targeted nerve, and inadvertently puncturing bone or tissue during the procedure have been ruled out. Additionally, the patient having pneumonia and dehydration have been ruled out. A Curonix Representative conducted a review of sterilization and packaging records for the respective product lot; Curonix has confirmed that the product was delivered sterile, validated sterilization parameters were used, and sterile barriers were verified to be intact following packaging. The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the swelling is unknown. The investigation findings do not lead to a clear conclusion about the cause of the reported issue. Therefore, conclusion has been selected as unable to determine root cause.Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Other Adverse Events Issues. Other Adverse Events Issues rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required. Other Adverse Events Issues rates will continue to be tracked and trended."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50885794",
  "narrative": "The Unintended Stimulation/New Pain Questionnaire was reviewed for potential causes. Based on this review, the patient having another non-Curonix device implanted has been ruled out. Other potential causes include: programming parameters, the patient having contraindicating conditions, severe force applied to the implant (patient fall, accident), and off-label use. The Transmitter Assembly associated with the complaint was returned for investigation. Investigation of the device was unable to replicate the alleged issue. Although the alleged issue was not replicated, the investigation found the RF connector was damaged caused by mishandling. The SMA RF connector broke off the PCB, triggering \"low power detect\" and/or \"antenna disconnect\" alarms. Therefore, the device cannot deliver therapy.The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the shocking sensation is unknown. Although the cause of the shocking sensation is unknown, the investigation found a Broken Connector (User Error-Patient). Refer to CAPA-2024-0020 for additional investigation details for the broken RF connector. Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Unintended Stimulation/New Pain issues. Unintended Stimulation/New Pain issue rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required. Unintended Stimulation/New Pain Issues rates will continue to be tracked and trended.\n\nThe patient reported a shocking sensation from their Transmitter Assembly. The patient was provided a replacement Transmitter Assembly and the shocking sensations were resolved."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50885184",
  "narrative": "The Surgical Issue Questionnaire was reviewed for potential causes of the reported issue. Based on this review, implanting the device after the expiration date, not prepping the surface of the skin with an antiseptic solution, and multiple tunneling attempts have been ruled out. Additionally, the patient touching or picking at the wound has been ruled out. However, the patient is a poor surgical risk as they are prone to infections. Per the Freedom Peripheral Nerve Stimulator System Implantation of Neurostimulator Instructions for Use, 05-20200 Rev. 8 \"Poor surgical risks - Peripheral nerve stimulators should not be used on patients who are poor surgical risks or patients with multiple illnesses, active general infections, or other potential surgical risks as identified by the clinician.\" Additionally, non-compliance to the IFU was identified as the surgical site was closed using staples. \"Closures are usually done in two steps, with absorbable sutures for the deeper layer and either nonabsorbable or absorbable sutures for the superficial layer.\"A Curonix Representative conducted a review of sterilization and packaging records for the respective product lot; Curonix has confirmed that the product was delivered sterile, validated sterilization parameters were used, and sterile barriers were verified to be intact following packaging. The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the infection is attributed to two identified root causes: Patient is a Poor Candidate Due to Health, Weight, Age, or Mental Capacity as they are prone to infections (User Error-Clinician.) Non-Compliance to the IFU as the surgical site was closed using staples (User Error-Clinician). Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Surgical Issues. Surgical Issue rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required at this time. Surgical Issue rates will continue to be tracked and trended.\n\nThe patient reported an infection at the receiver site with clear liquid discharge off and on. Antibiotics were prescribed and an explant procedure was approved. Several attempts have been made to schedule the explant procedure. However, the patient refuses to move forward and does not want to take the device out. The physician is planning to speak to the patient again. No further information is available at this time."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50872338",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The implanted trial leads are designed and intended to be removed as part of the normal trial process. The removal of the leads is normally performed in a clinical setting, however in this case the patient was able to remove them independently with instructions from the implanting physician. The patient did not report any ongoing pain or physical issues after removing the trial leads. The patient reported no signs of infection at the surgical incision site. The device was discarded by the patient and not available for evaluation. Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined.\n\nThe Nalu Representative (rep) reported that during the trial implantation, after the first lead was placed at the sciatic nerve, the patient did not feel paresthesia in the region of her pain. A second lead was placed at the common peroneal nerve, and the patient was discharged to the recovery room. At this time, the patient still did not achieve paresthesia in the region of her pain, and the plan was to attempt to achieve this with programming. Ultimately, the patient reported paresthesia in the region of her pain after programming, although she was reporting post-procedure pain at this time. The nurse dressed the area and provided thorough wound care instructions. At the time the patient was discharged, and it was our understanding that she was achieving paresthesia in the region of her pain. Two hours later, the patient reported severe pain that was affecting her ability to walk and was unwilling to return to the study site at that time because she was already an hour away. The patient felt at that time that her pain was attributed to the lead at the sciatic nerve. She was provided with instructions on removing the sciatic lead by the physician. After this conversation, there was no communication from the patient. The patient had removed the second lead due to discomfort."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50871705",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The reported device remains implanted. The patient is continuing to receive pain relief therapy from the system for 5 months after that time with no issues. Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined.\n\nThe Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that the patient called and stated that he was working and he had a massive back spasm in his work truck, and he went home from work. The patient called the next day and stated that he went to the ER, had a CT scan, and it was unremarkable. The patient stated that these spasms happen when the disc is both providing therapy and is off his body as well. The patient has been seen by the implanting physician subsequent to the spasm episode and it was noted that the spasm subsided with no intervention and was instructed to reach out to Nalu if he needed any adjustments to his therapy. There was no reported patient harm or injury."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50869437",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. This incident is being re-opened/updated per PCAR42 remediation activities. No device has been returned to the firm as it remains implanted; therefore, the cause of the reported symptoms could not be determined based on the available information.\n\nThe patient reported to a Nalu sales representative feeling pain in the trapezoid area. Images showed the Implantable Pulse Generator (IPG) and Leads were in good position.The patient reported they have multiple \"situations\" going on with various pain issues. They are having a difficult time locating a physician in the current new area they moved to who will address the issues and assist with patient's request for explant of the Nalu system. Patient reports not using the Nalu system at this time and currently has no plans for intervention due to lack of doctor care. No additional information was provided."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50869095",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that the patient lost therapy in the medial lead and imaging found that lead is also fractured. The cause of second lead fracture is unknown at this time; however the patient has a history of falls, and it is suspected that a fall is directly related to the most recent lead fracture. The Nalu Sales Rep and the physician's office have attempted to communicate with the patient on multiple occasions and patient has remained unresponsive since the initial report of a fractured lead. The patient no longer has therapy. The physician has not decided how he will proceed with the patient's treatment.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The reported device remains implanted. Xray images supplied by the physician confirm the fracture. The reported device was not returned for evaluation; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50869092",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that an impedance reading while programming and the lateral lead showed all four contacts as being red. Imaging was done and the lead appears to have a fracture. The patient sustained a fall and subsequently lost therapy from the lateral lead. The rep programmed the patient to see if coverage and relief could be established with the remaining lead. There was no reported patient harm or injury.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42. The reported device remains implanted. Xray images supplied by the physician confirm the fracture. The reported device was not returned for evaluation; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50867972",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported impedance issues during implant procedure as well as during initial activation and programming, so she tried to utilize tight bipoles as close to the PSP algorithm suggested locations on lead B as possible. The patient seemed to have decent coverage and went out of town after that programming. No equipment was replaced and all components remained implanted. There was no reported patient harm or injury.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42. The reported device remains implanted, and the rep has not heard any complaints from the patient at this time. Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50867030",
  "narrative": "The Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that when the physician placed an EIC on the lead, that all contacts showed bad impedances. Troubleshooting was done and the lead continued to show bad impedances. The Clincial Programmer app was restarted and there were now only a couple of contacts that were bad and programing was attempted. The patient was not feeling the stimulation, and the physician repositioned the lead. The physician removed the lead and put it in the other port, and as he did, the top of the ESC snapped in two, and a new one was used. The case continued with new lead and a new EIC and the case was completed without issue.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.All malfunctioning leads were discarded during the procedure and thus are not available for inspection by the firm. There were multiple people handling the devices during the procedure and reports of multiple attempts at connecting and reconnecting the components. Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50858500",
  "narrative": "It was reported during an implant procedure there was an unknown problem inserting the lead into the IPG. A second lead was attempted and eventually successful. High impedances were being seen with both leads. Procedure was eventually successful. No reports were received of significant delay to procedure and no harm to patient or staff.\n\nAfter conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. This incident is being re-opened/updated per PCAR42 remediation activities. The device evaluation was unable to be completed. The devices were confirmed and receipt of decontaminated lead and IPG. Subsequently the IPG was inadvertently discarded."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50842915",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. This incident is being re-opened/updated per PCAR42 remediation activities. No product was able to be salvaged from this case; therefore, the cause of the reported symptoms could not be determined based on the available information.MDR no. 3015425075-2026-00730 will address Lead UDI: (b)(4) .\n\nIt was reported that during trial procedure, both leads were showing red ohms. Both leads were wiped multiple times, The Electrode Interfacing Cable (EIC) and EIC extension were swapped and issue persisted. New leads were opened, and the red ohms cleared."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50842912",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. This incident is being re-opened/updated per PCAR42 remediation activities. No product was able to be salvaged from this case; therefore, the cause of the reported symptoms could not be determined based on the available information.MDR no. 3015425075-2026-00780 will address Lead UDI: (b)(4) .\n\nIt was reported that during trial procedure, both leads were showing red ohms. Both leads were wiped multiple times, The Electrode Interfacing Cable (EIC) and EIC extension were swapped and issue persisted. New leads were opened, and the red ohms cleared."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50840744",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42.The reported device was returned for evaluation. Visual inspection found that there is an exposed wire poking out of a slice in the connector boot. On the opposing side, there is a similar slice facing the same direction, as well as a slice near the pushing tool port. The damage to the connector boot appears to have been done with an object akin to forceps or scissors. The device was likely damaged during surgery due to components that are sensitive to handling with surgical tools.\n\nThe Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that a defective Implantable Pulse Generator (IPG) was discovered during perm implant. After the leads were placed and tested, impedance and paresthesia mapping were good. Moved on to tunnel the leads. After leads were tunneled, moved on to hooking up the IPG and leads. Impedance check did not clear on contact B4. Physician wanted to give IPG a depth test with therapy disc to see if there were any other issues with the IPG. During the pocketing procedure physician noticed what looked like a piece of wire protruding from the tail of the IPG. Upon closer inspection, there was in fact wire hanging out of the tail. The physician and Nalu rep both agreed that this IPG should not be utilized due to the bad impedance reading on contact B4 and the wire protruding from the tail of the IPG. A new IPG was used to complete the case. There was no reported patient harm or injury."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50839747",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42 remediation activities.The device remains implanted; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined.\n\nThe Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that the patient noticed a lump on the patient's skin at the IPG implant site. It appears that the lead may be backing out. Impedance check was run and there are high impedances across all contacts. The patient may be without therapy. Follow up with the patient noted that he did not see his doctor but says that the system is working well and has no issues. There was no reported patient harm or injury."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50839514",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42 remediation activities.The Therapy Disc was returned for evaluation. The TD passed ATE and did not show signs of magnetization or increased RF power. Evaluation was unable to reproduce the reported amplitude and shock issue; Therefore, the root cause of the reported event could not be determined.\n\nThe Nalu Sales Representative (rep) reported that the patient said that he's having issues with his Therapy Disc (TD), that the Amplitude settings are changing and giving him shocks. No further information was provided. There was no reported patient harm or injury."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50812902",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42 remediation activities.Multiple attempts were made to obtain the surgical tools used for this procedure. The reported device was not returned for evaluation. Therefore, the root cause cannot be determined.\n\nThe Nalu representative reported that when the patient was receiving PNS, dual 8-contact lead system for occipital PNS. The patient was positioned on the table prior to physician entering the room and anesthesia was already administered. When the physician entered the room, he noted that the patient was not in the preferred positioning with regard to her neck being in extension, not flexion. After the patient was repositioned with a bolster under the chest, the physician began the case. The product was opened and two leads were placed on the appropriate nerve targets. Impedances were checked and all within range. When the physician attempted to tunnel to the upper back, the physician explained to the anesthesiologist that the patient's neck was not in the preferred position. The physician requested that the patient's position be adjusted, but when the anesthesiologist dropped the patient's head lower, anesthesiologist notified the physician that he had lost the patient's airway. The tunneling was attempted one more time but deemed to be a risk to the patients safety, due to an airway and positioning issue. The decision was made to pull the leads and abort the case without implant. There was no reported patient harm or injury."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50808902",
  "narrative": "The Surgical Issue Questionnaire was reviewed for potential causes of the reported issue. However, the patient is on blood thinners and was not instructed to stop them which likely contributed to the bleeding after the implant procedure. A Curonix Representative conducted a review of sterilization and packaging records for the respective product lot; Curonix has confirmed that the product was delivered sterile, validated sterilization parameters were used, and sterile barriers were verified to be intact following packaging. The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the bleeding after the implant procedure is Unrelated to the Curonix Device as the patient was on blood thinners and was not instructed to stop them (No Fault Found). Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Surgical Issues. Surgical Issue rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required at this time. Surgical Issue rates will continue to be tracked and trended.\n\nThe patient reported bleeding at the incision site after their trial implant procedure. The patient went to the hospital, the bandages were removed, the STERIS-Strips were left in tact, pressure was applied to stop the bleeding, and the wound was redressed in order for the patient to continue the trial. As of (b)(6)2026, the bleeding has stopped."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50804957",
  "narrative": "The Surgical Issue Questionnaire was reviewed for potential causes of the reported issue. Based on this review, implanting the device after the expiration date, not prepping the surface of the skin with an antiseptic solution, not irrigating the incision site with an antibiotic solution before closure, multiple tunneling attempts, using incorrect tools, and not prescribing antibiotics pre-operatively have been ruled out. Additionally, improperly closing the incision site and the patient having contraindicating conditions have been ruled out. A Curonix Representative conducted a review of sterilization and packaging records for the respective product lot; Curonix has confirmed that the product was delivered sterile, validated sterilization parameters were used, and sterile barriers were verified to be intact following packaging. The stimulator is used to treat pain. The cause of the infection is unknown. The investigation findings do not lead to a clear conclusion about the cause of the reported issue. Therefore, conclusion has been selected as unable to determine root cause.Rates reviewed at the most recent Complaint Trending meeting do not indicate a significant increase in Surgical Issues. Surgical Issue rates remain acceptably low; thus, a CAPA is not required at this time. Surgical Issue rates will continue to be tracked and trended.\n\nThe patient reported their distal incision at the receiver site was opening and started to show signs of infection with redness, pus, and odor. Antibiotics were prescribed and an incision washout was performed on (b)(6) 2026. As of (b)(6) 2026, the patient is fine. No further information is available at this time."
 },
 {
  "mdr_report_key": "50792854",
  "narrative": "After conducting a post-acquisition, retrospective review of Nalu Peripheral Nerve Stimulation complaint records, Boston Scientific determined that this event meets MDR reporting criteria. The following MDR information is being submitted as part of CAPA investigation PCAR42 remediation activities.There does not appear to be any correlation between the patient's mental status and the Nalu trial. There is no confirmation of a cardiac event, only patient assertion. The reported device was not returned for evaluation. Therefore, the root cause cannot be determined.\n\nThe Nalu Representative reported that the patient had been missing for several days of their Nalu trial and discovered that the patient was admitted to a care facility via the Baker Act. And the patient reportedly remains in a medical facility, possibly having also sustained a cardiac event. The Nalu trial leads were removed. There is no confirmation of a cardiac event, only patient assertion. Nalu trial leads were removed as designed."
 }
]
